CertHub.register({"id":"az-900","vendor":"Microsoft","name":"Microsoft Certified: Azure Fundamentals","short":"Azure Fundamentals","exam":"AZ-900","blurb":"Entry-level exam for anyone new to the cloud or to Azure, technical or not: cloud concepts and service models, Azure's core architecture, compute, networking, storage, identity and security services, and the tools for managing cost, governance and compliance.","status":"check","statusNote":"Microsoft Learn (learn.microsoft.com) cannot be opened from this environment, so nothing here was read on the official page during this task. The three skill areas and their ranges come from the published AZ-900 study guide as best known: Describe cloud concepts 25–30%; Describe Azure architecture and services 35–40%; Describe Azure management and governance 30–35%. The midpoints (27.5/37.5/32.5) were scaled and rounded to 27/38/35 so they sum to 100. Confirm the skills measured, the change log at the end of the study guide, the exam length and the price on the official pages before you book.","lastVerified":"2026-09-25","notices":[],"examInfo":{"questions":"Not published (Microsoft fundamentals exams usually have about 40–60 questions, which can include multiple choice, drag-and-drop and yes/no series)","minutes":45,"pass":"700 on a 1–1000 scale","extra":"Fundamentals certifications do not expire, so there is no yearly renewal. No prerequisites. Microsoft Learn offers a free self-paced learning path and a free practice assessment for AZ-900."},"examSim":{"questions":45,"minutes":45},"sources":[{"label":"Microsoft Learn: Microsoft Certified: Azure Fundamentals","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/azure-fundamentals/"},{"label":"Microsoft Learn: Study guide for Exam AZ-900 (skills measured)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/az-900"},{"label":"Microsoft Learn: Course AZ-900T00 Microsoft Azure Fundamentals","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/courses/az-900t00"}],"planWeeks":6,"hoursPerWeek":"4–6","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Describe cloud concepts","w":27,"topics":["What cloud computing is, and the shared responsibility model across on-premises, IaaS, PaaS and SaaS","Cloud models: public, private and hybrid cloud, plus multicloud and where Azure Arc fits","The consumption-based model and pay-as-you-go pricing compared with buying hardware","Capital expenditure (CapEx) vs operational expenditure (OpEx) and how the cloud shifts spending","High availability, service-level agreements (SLAs) and composite availability","Scalability and elasticity: scaling up vs scaling out, manual vs automatic","Reliability and predictability of performance and cost in the cloud","Security, governance and manageability benefits of the cloud (management of the cloud vs in the cloud)","Infrastructure as a service (IaaS): what you manage and typical use cases such as lift-and-shift","Platform as a service (PaaS) and serverless: what the provider manages and typical use cases","Software as a service (SaaS) and choosing between IaaS, PaaS and SaaS for a scenario"],"notes":["Skills measured: Describe cloud computing; Describe the benefits of using cloud services; Describe cloud service types"],"labs":["No account needed: draw a four-column table (on-premises, IaaS, PaaS, SaaS) and mark who manages each layer: data, identities and accounts, devices, applications, network controls, operating system, physical hosts, network and datacenter. Then compare it with the shared responsibility diagram in the free Microsoft Learn AZ-900 learning path.","Free: open the Azure Pricing Calculator (no sign-in needed) and price one B1s Linux VM for a month in two different regions, then again with a 1-year reservation. Write down how pay-as-you-go, reservations and region change the monthly OpEx figure compared with buying a server outright.","Free Azure account or a Microsoft Learn sandbox: create a Free (F1) App Service web app from the portal quickstart, look at what you did not have to configure (OS, patching, load balancing), and compare with creating a B1s VM. Cost warning: stay on F1 and B1s (free-tier sizes), and delete the resource group the same day so nothing keeps using credit."]},{"id":2,"name":"Describe Azure architecture and services","w":38,"topics":["Azure regions, region pairs and sovereign regions (Azure Government, Azure operated by 21Vianet in China)","Availability zones and datacenters: zonal vs zone-redundant services","Azure resources, resource groups, subscriptions and management groups: the hierarchy and what each is for","Compute: virtual machines, VM scale sets, availability sets and Azure Virtual Desktop","Containers and serverless compute: Container Instances, Container Apps, AKS, Azure Functions and App Service","Virtual networks, subnets, peering, Azure DNS, and public vs private endpoints","Hybrid connectivity: VPN Gateway (site-to-site, point-to-site) vs ExpressRoute","Azure Storage services (Blob, Files, Queue, Table, Disks), storage account types and access tiers (Hot, Cool, Cold, Archive)","Storage redundancy: LRS, ZRS, GRS, GZRS and read-access secondary options","Moving data and migrating: AzCopy, Storage Explorer, Azure File Sync, Azure Migrate and Azure Data Box","Microsoft Entra ID and Entra Domain Services; authentication methods: SSO, MFA and passwordless","External identities (B2B and customer identity) and Conditional Access","Azure role-based access control (RBAC), Zero Trust, defense in depth and Microsoft Defender for Cloud"],"notes":["Skills measured: Describe the core architectural components of Azure; Describe Azure compute and networking services; Describe Azure storage services; Describe Azure identity, access, and security"],"labs":["Free Azure account: create a resource group, then a Standard LRS StorageV2 account in it; upload a text file to a blob container, change the blob's access tier to Cool, and look at the redundancy options on the account's Redundancy page (do not switch to geo-redundant). Cost warning: storage is tiny but not free after the credit, so delete the resource group when finished.","Free Azure account: create two virtual networks with non-overlapping ranges (10.1.0.0/16 and 10.2.0.0/16), peer them, and view the peering status and each VNet's subnets. VNets and peering setup cost nothing when no traffic flows; skip VPN Gateway and ExpressRoute, which bill by the hour. Delete the resource group afterward.","Free: in Microsoft Entra ID (the Free tier that comes with any Azure account) create a test user and a security group, turn on MFA for your own account with the Microsoft Authenticator app, and assign the group the Reader role on a resource group; sign in as the test user to confirm they can view but not change resources. Then open Microsoft Defender for Cloud's free tier and read the Secure Score recommendations."]},{"id":3,"name":"Describe Azure management and governance","w":35,"topics":["Factors that affect cost in Azure: resource type, consumption, region, bandwidth, reservations and Azure Hybrid Benefit","The Pricing Calculator vs the Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) Calculator","Microsoft Cost Management: cost analysis, budgets and alerts, and using tags to track spending","Microsoft Purview for data governance, and the Service Trust Portal for compliance reports","Azure Policy: definitions, initiatives, assignments and compliance","Resource locks: CanNotDelete vs ReadOnly and how they inherit","Tools for interacting with Azure: the portal, Azure Cloud Shell, Azure CLI and Azure PowerShell","Azure Arc for managing on-premises and multicloud resources","Azure Resource Manager and infrastructure as code with ARM templates and Bicep","Azure Advisor recommendations and Azure Service Health (Azure status, Service Health, Resource Health)","Azure Monitor: metrics, Log Analytics, alerts and Application Insights"],"notes":["Skills measured: Describe cost management in Azure; Describe features and tools in Azure for governance and compliance; Describe features and tools for managing and deploying Azure resources; Describe monitoring tools in Azure"],"labs":["Free Azure account: in Cost Management create a budget of $5 on your subscription with an email alert at 50% and 100%, then open Cost analysis and group costs by resource group. Add a tag CostCenter=Lab to a resource group and filter cost analysis by that tag. Budgets and tags are free.","Free Azure account: assign the built-in Azure Policy 'Allowed locations' to a resource group with only one region allowed, try to create a storage account in another region and read the Deny message; then add a CanNotDelete lock to the group, try to delete it, remove the lock and delete it. Policy and locks cost nothing; delete any storage account you created.","Free: open Azure Cloud Shell from the portal (it asks to create a small storage account; choose the option without storage if offered, or delete that account later) and run `az group list -o table` in Bash and `Get-AzResourceGroup` in PowerShell. Then open Azure Advisor and Service Health and read what they show for your subscription. Cost warning: stay within free services and delete anything you created."]}],"study":{"1":[["Explain the shared responsibility model in one sentence per service type.","On-premises you manage everything. In IaaS Microsoft runs the physical datacenter, network and hosts, and you manage the OS, apps and data. In PaaS Microsoft also manages the OS and runtime, and you manage apps and data. In SaaS Microsoft runs the application too; you always keep responsibility for your data, devices, accounts and identities."],["What is the difference between public, private and hybrid cloud?","Public cloud is run by a provider and shared by many customers over the internet, with no upfront hardware. Private cloud is used by one organization, on its own premises or hosted, with more control but hardware costs. Hybrid combines them so workloads can run in either place. Multicloud means using more than one public provider; Azure Arc lets you manage those resources from Azure."],["Contrast CapEx and OpEx and say which the cloud uses.","CapEx is spending up front on physical assets such as servers and datacenters, which are then depreciated. OpEx is ongoing spending on services as you use them. The cloud's consumption-based model is OpEx: no upfront cost, you pay for what you use and stop paying when you stop using it."],["What is the difference between scaling up and scaling out, and what is elasticity?","Scaling up (vertical) gives one resource more CPU or memory. Scaling out (horizontal) adds more instances. Elasticity is scaling automatically, both out and back in, as demand changes, so you only pay for capacity when you need it."],["What does an SLA describe, and how does adding a nine change downtime?","An SLA is Microsoft's commitment to a service's uptime and connectivity, usually as a percentage per month, with service credits if it is missed. 99.9% allows about 43 minutes of downtime a month, 99.99% about 4 minutes. Combining services that each have an SLA gives a composite SLA lower than each one."],["When would you choose IaaS, PaaS or SaaS?","IaaS for lift-and-shift, custom OS needs, or test and dev environments where you want the most control. PaaS when developers want to focus on code while the provider runs the OS and runtime (App Service, Azure SQL Database, Functions). SaaS when you just want a finished application such as Microsoft 365 email."]],"2":[["What are region pairs and why do they matter?","Most Azure regions are paired with another region in the same geography, usually at least 300 miles apart. Planned platform updates roll out to one region of a pair at a time, recovery of one region in a pair is prioritized during a broad outage, and geo-redundant storage replicates to the paired region. Some newer regions have no pair and rely on availability zones."],["What is an availability zone, and what is the difference between zonal and zone-redundant services?","An availability zone is one or more physically separate datacenters within a region, with independent power, cooling and networking. Zonal services (such as a VM) are pinned to a zone you choose; zone-redundant services (such as ZRS storage) are spread across zones automatically."],["Describe the Azure resource hierarchy.","Management groups contain subscriptions (and other management groups); subscriptions contain resource groups; resource groups contain resources. Each resource belongs to exactly one resource group. Policies and RBAC assignments applied at a higher level are inherited by everything below it."],["Compare VPN Gateway and ExpressRoute.","VPN Gateway sends encrypted traffic over the public internet (site-to-site for offices, point-to-site for single devices). ExpressRoute is a private connection through a connectivity provider that does not go over the public internet, giving more reliability, lower latency and higher bandwidth at a higher cost."],["What is the difference between LRS, ZRS, GRS and GZRS?","LRS keeps three copies in one datacenter. ZRS keeps three copies across availability zones in one region. GRS keeps LRS in the primary region plus three copies in the paired region. GZRS combines ZRS in the primary region with a copy in the paired region. The RA- versions let you read from the secondary region at any time."],["How do authentication, authorization, Conditional Access and RBAC relate?","Authentication proves who you are (Microsoft Entra ID with passwords, MFA or passwordless methods). Conditional Access uses signals such as location, device and risk to decide whether to allow sign-in or require MFA. Authorization decides what you can do: Azure RBAC grants roles such as Reader or Contributor at a scope."]],"3":[["Which factors change what you pay in Azure?","The resource type and size, how much you consume, the region, outbound bandwidth (inbound data is generally free), the purchase model (pay-as-you-go, reservations, savings plans, spot), Azure Hybrid Benefit for existing licenses, and whether you use Azure Marketplace products."],["Pricing Calculator or TCO Calculator: which, when?","The Pricing Calculator estimates the monthly cost of Azure services you plan to deploy. The TCO Calculator compares the total cost of running your current on-premises workloads, including hardware, power, cooling and labor, with running them in Azure over several years."],["What is the difference between Azure Policy, RBAC and resource locks?","RBAC controls who can perform actions. Azure Policy controls what resources and settings are allowed and reports compliance, no matter who deploys. Locks stop resources from being deleted (CanNotDelete) or changed (ReadOnly), even by users whose roles would allow it."],["What does Azure Arc do?","It projects servers, Kubernetes clusters and data services that run outside Azure (on-premises or in other clouds) into Azure Resource Manager, so you can manage them with the same tools: Azure Policy, RBAC, tags, monitoring and Defender for Cloud."],["Why use infrastructure as code with ARM templates or Bicep?","Templates are declarative: you describe the resources you want and Azure Resource Manager works out the order and deploys them. Deployments are repeatable, consistent across environments, can be kept in source control and reviewed like code. Bicep is a simpler language that compiles to ARM JSON."],["Compare Azure Advisor, Service Health and Azure Monitor.","Advisor gives personalized recommendations for reliability, security, performance, operational excellence and cost. Service Health tells you about Azure outages, planned maintenance and health advisories that affect your services and regions, with Resource Health for individual resources. Azure Monitor collects metrics and logs, runs alerts, and includes Log Analytics and Application Insights."]]},"qCount":150,"hasLessons":true,"hasPbqs":true,"hasLevels":true});
